{"command": "cd /testbed && echo '=== spec.md (FR/SC/US) ===' && cat specs/001-transactions/spec.md && echo '=== constitution? ===' && (cat .specify/memory/constitution.md 2>/dev/null | head -40 || echo 'NO constitution')", "exit_code": 0, "stderr": "", "stdout": "=== spec.md (FR/SC/US) ===\n# Feature Specification: Add Two Numbers Utility\n\n**Feature Branch**: `001-transactions`\n\n**Created**: 2026-10-16\n\n**Status**: Draft\n\n**Input**: User description: \"Unscored calibration. R01: create smoke.py with add(a,b) returning a+b. R02: support negative integers, zero and floats. R03: add and run tests. Do not modify TinyDB. Keep workflow artifacts concise under specs/001-transactions. No service or deployment is involved.\"\n\n## User Scenarios & Testing *(mandatory)*\n\n### User Story 1 - Add two numbers (Priority: P1)\n\nA consumer asks the utility to add two numbers and receives their sum back,\nwith no other side effects.\n\n**Why this priority**: This is the core, minimum viable behavior of the\nfeature; everything else builds on it.\n\n**Independent Test**: Can be fully tested by requesting the sum of two known\nnumbers (for example 1 and 2) and confirming the result is 3.\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n\n1. **Given** the utility is available, **When** it is asked to add 1 and 2,\n   **Then** it returns 3.\n2. **Given** the utility is available, **When** it is asked to add 5 and 7,\n   **Then** it returns 12.\n\n---\n\n### User Story 2 - Handle negative integers, zero, and floats (Priority: P1)\n\nA consumer can add negative integers, zero, and float numbers and always\nreceives the correct arithmetic sum.\n\n**Why this priority**: The stated number coverage is part of the core contract\nand must hold for the same primary flow, not a later enhancement.\n\n**Independent Test**: Can be fully tested by adding representative pairs from\neach supported category (negative + positive, zero + number, float + float,\nnegative + float) and confirming each expected sum.\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n\n1. **Given** the utility is available, **When** it is asked to add -3 and 5,\n   **Then** it returns 2.\n2. **Given** the utility is available, **When** it is asked to add 0 and 4,\n   **Then** it returns 4.\n3. **Given** the utility is available, **When** it is asked to add 1.5 and 2.25,\n   **Then** it returns 3.75.\n4. **Given** the utility is available, **When** it is asked to add -1.5 and 0.5,\n   **Then** it returns -1.0.\n\n---\n\n### User Story 3 - Verified by automated tests (Priority: P2)\n\nA maintainer runs the automated test suite and sees the addition behavior\nverified end to end, so regressions are caught immediately.\n\n**Why this priority**: Verification proves the P1 behavior; it adds no new\nuser-facing capability but is required for confidence and for the R03\nrequirement.\n\n**Independent Test**: Can be fully tested by executing the project test runner\nand observing that all addition tests pass with no failures.\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n\n1. **Given** the utility and its tests exist, **When** the test suite is run,\n   **Then** every test for the addition behavior passes.\n\n---\n\n### Edge Cases\n\n- What happens when both inputs are negative? The sum is the negative of their\n  magnitudes (e.g., -2 + -3 = -5).\n- What happens when zero is added to a float? The float is returned unchanged\n  in value (e.g., 0 + 2.5 = 2.5).\n- What happens with float results that are not exactly representable? Results\n  are compared with standard floating-point equality on the test inputs chosen\n  so that exact sums are expected (e.g., 1.5 + 2.25 = 3.75).\n- Unsupported input types (strings, missing arguments) are out of scope; no\n  validation behavior is specified.\n\n## Requirements *(mandatory)*\n\n### Functional Requirements\n\n- **FR-001**: The utility MUST provide an addition operation that takes two\n  numeric arguments and returns their arithmetic sum.\n- **FR-002**: The addition operation MUST return a+b exactly for integer\n  inputs (e.g., add(1, 2) = 3).\n- **FR-003**: The addition operation MUST support negative integer inputs\n  (e.g., add(-3, 5) = 2).\n- **FR-004**: The addition operation MUST support zero as an input\n  (e.g., add(0, 4) = 4).\n- **FR-005**: The addition operation MUST support float inputs, including\n  mixed int/float pairs (e.g., add(1.5, 2.25) = 3.75; add(1, 2.5) = 3.5).\n- **FR-006**: Automated tests MUST exist for the addition behavior (FR-001\n  through FR-005) and MUST be runnable by the project's existing test runner.\n- **FR-007**: The implementation MUST NOT modify the existing TinyDB package\n  or its tests.\n\n## Success Criteria *(mandatory)*\n\n### Measurable Outcomes\n\n- **SC-001**: 100% of the specified addition scenarios (FR-001 through FR-005)\n  return the exact expected sum when exercised.\n- **SC-002**: The automated test suite passes with 0 failures when run from\n  the repository root.\n- **SC-003**: A consumer obtains the sum of two supported numbers in a single\n  call with no additional setup, configuration, or side effects.\n- **SC-004**: The feature is delivered as a single small utility module plus\n  its tests, with no service, deployment, or new external dependencies.\n\n## Assumptions\n\n- The utility is a plain in-process module (smoke.py at the repository root),\n  not a service, CLI, or library distribution; no deployment is involved.\n- \"Supports\" negative integers, zero, and floats means these types are accepted\n  inputs and produce the correct sum; no error-handling contract is defined for\n  unsupported types.\n- The existing project test runner (pytest) is the mechanism for \"add and run\n  tests\"; no new tooling is introduced.\n- Float correctness is judged with standard equality on inputs whose sums are\n  exactly representable (e.g., 1.5 + 2.25 = 3.75).\n- The feature stays within the existing Python standard library; no new\n  dependencies.\n- The TinyDB package and its tests remain untouched (per R01 constraint and\n  constitution principle I).\n=== constitution? ===\n<!--\nSync Impact Report (temporary scratch for amendment review; remove before commit)\n- Version change: 0.0.0 (unratified scaffold) -> 1.0.0\n- Modified principles: none (initial ratification; all scaffold placeholders replaced)\n- Added sections: Core Principles (I-V), Scope Constraints, Development Workflow, Governance\n- Removed sections: none\n- Deferred items: none\n-->\n\n# Arithmetic Utility Constitution\n\n## Core Principles\n\n### I. Preserve Existing Public API Behavior\n\nChanges MUST NOT alter the observable behavior of existing public APIs. The\nupstream TinyDB package is out of scope and MUST NOT be modified. New code\n(e.g., smoke.py) MUST expose only the behavior specified for the feature.\n\nRationale: protects downstream consumers and keeps the calibration task scoped\nto the arithmetic utility.\n\n### II. Testable Acceptance Criteria\n\nEvery requirement MUST carry at least one testable, verifiable acceptance\ncriterion before implementation begins (e.g., add(1, 2) returns 3; add supports\nnegative integers, zero, and floats).\n\nRationale: untestable requirements are ambiguous and cannot be verified.\n\n### III. Evidence Over Assertion\n\nClaims about behavior MUST be backed by observed evidence (test output,\ncommand results). Unverified assertions MUST be explicitly labeled as\nunverified rather than reported as facts.\n\nRationale: hidden acceptance is unavailable; only observed results count.\n\n### IV. Simple, Scoped Implementations\n\n"}